WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:06.400
So you're sitting on the couch, staring at
the Netflix menu, once again frozen with indecision.

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:10.769
Life shouldn't have to be this hard — and
we're here to make it just a little easier.

00:00:10.769 --> 00:00:14.759
From incredible underrated gems to timeless
favorites that you haven't thought about in

00:00:14.759 --> 00:00:18.220
years, we've rounded up the best movies on
Netflix right now.

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:20.130
The Shining

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:26.460
What can you say about The Shining that hasn't
been said, oh, 237 times before?

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:28.420
"Here's Johnny!"

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:34.540
Even though it's almost 40 years old, Stanley
Kubrick's horror tour de force is still delivering

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:37.120
fresh chills and thrills to new generations.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:42.790
Part of the reason is that homicidal psychosis
doesn't age the way bad CG and monster suits

00:00:42.790 --> 00:00:47.510
do — Jack's slow spiral into insanity at
the Overlook Hotel rings just as true now

00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:49.330
as it did in 1980.

00:00:49.330 --> 00:00:53.620
Not only that, but The Shining is also just
damn entertaining — it's one of Kubrick's

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:58.100
best movies, one of Jack Nicholson's best
movies, and one of the best horror movies

00:00:58.100 --> 00:00:59.760
ever made.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:01.280
My Left Foot

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:05.800
Daniel Day-Lewis earned his first Academy
Award for his performance in My Left Foot,

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:11.530
a powerful biopic about real-life writer Christy
Brown, a lifelong quadriplegic who could only

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:13.360
write using his toes.

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:17.970
Day-Lewis masterfully portrays all the nuances
and frustrations born of a life confined to

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:23.670
a wheelchair — no doubt thanks to the fact
that he Method-acted the crap out of the role,

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:27.600
refusing to leave the wheelchair or break
character between takes.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:32.070
That's insane dedication, but it paid off
— My Left Foot is an immensely emotional

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:36.590
movie about one man's triumph over the cruel
hand dealt to him at birth.

00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:38.030
Hot Fuzz

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:44.670
Edgar Wright's 2007 hit Hot Fuzz is arguably
the best of the so-called "Cornetto Trilogy,"

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:47.920
which also includes Shaun of the Dead and
The World's End.

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:52.690
The story starts with Simon Pegg's super-cop
Nicholas Angel, a London policeman who's so

00:01:52.690 --> 00:01:56.590
good at his job that he's making the rest
of the department look bad.

00:01:56.590 --> 00:02:01.140
To save their image, the brass reassigns him
to a sleepy little town in the English countryside.

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:05.840
"If we let you carry on, running around town,
you'll continue to be exceptional, and we

00:02:05.841 --> 00:02:07.340
can't have that."

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:11.909
Of course, it's only a matter of time before
he uncovers an insidious plot hiding beneath

00:02:11.909 --> 00:02:13.900
the perfect façade.

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:17.909
Inspired by every action movie ever and packed
with more jokes than a stand-up special, Hot

00:02:17.909 --> 00:02:23.409
Fuzz is one of the best action comedies ever
made, hands down.

00:02:23.409 --> 00:02:24.579
Spotlight

00:02:24.579 --> 00:02:29.170
Based on the true story of the Boston Globe's
investigation into allegations of widespread

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:34.189
sexual abuse within the Catholic Church, Spotlight
is one of those rare films that ignores the

00:02:34.189 --> 00:02:37.829
sensational and dives straight into the heart
of the story.

00:02:37.829 --> 00:02:42.159
With a sharp script and brilliant cast full
of scene-stealers, it's no wonder Spotlight

00:02:42.159 --> 00:02:46.950
took Best Picture and Best Original Screenplay
at the 2016 Oscars.

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:51.140
Like the Pulitzer-prize winning reporting
that inspired it, Spotlight is unflinching

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:55.310
and thorough in its take on a highly controversial
subject.

00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:56.349
Hush

00:02:56.349 --> 00:03:00.819
Currently sitting on Rotten Tomatoes with
a 94 percent rating as of the making of this

00:03:00.819 --> 00:03:06.459
video, 2016's Hush is an ingenious, white-knuckle
horror thriller that you can't miss.

00:03:06.459 --> 00:03:12.200
The premise is simple: Maddie, a deaf/mute
author, is staying in her isolated house deep

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:15.829
in the Alabama woods when a masked killer
appears at her window.

00:03:15.829 --> 00:03:21.060
Without saying a word, largely unknown actress
Kate Siegel portrays Maddie as strong, capable,

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:26.129
and intelligent — a breath of fresh air
for a female role in a horror movie.

00:03:26.129 --> 00:03:27.709
Nightcrawler

00:03:27.709 --> 00:03:32.859
Starring Jake Gyllenhaal in one of his most
quietly sinister roles, Nightcrawler follows

00:03:32.859 --> 00:03:37.529
Lou Bloom, a desperate young man who discovers
that he can make some quick cash by filming

00:03:37.529 --> 00:03:41.579
crime scenes and selling the footage to the
local news stations.

00:03:41.579 --> 00:03:46.760
Bloom gets sucked into the seedy world and
begins an ambitious, grueling climb to become

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:49.579
top dog in a world of bottom feeders.

00:03:49.579 --> 00:03:53.819
"With Los Angeles crime rates going down,
I think that makes items like mine particularly

00:03:53.819 --> 00:03:56.790
valuable, like rare animals."

00:03:56.790 --> 00:04:01.860
Gyllenhaal is fantastic, and first-time director
Dan Gilroy captures the grimy underbelly of

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:08.339
Los Angeles perfectly, evoking a sense of
disquiet and barely-concealed rage that surges

00:04:08.339 --> 00:04:12.239
just beneath the surface of both the city
and its inhabitants.

00:04:12.239 --> 00:04:14.099
No Country For Old Men

00:04:14.099 --> 00:04:20.329
Based on the 2005 Cormac McCarthy novel of
the same name, 2008's Oscar-sweeping No Country

00:04:20.329 --> 00:04:26.139
For Old Men takes place in the 1980s in Texas,
where a blue-collar worker named Llewelyn

00:04:26.139 --> 00:04:30.879
Moss, played by Josh Brolin, stumbles across
the aftermath of a violent drug shoot-out

00:04:30.879 --> 00:04:32.230
in the middle of the desert.

00:04:32.230 --> 00:04:37.490
Moss helps himself to a briefcase full of
cash, unwittingly putting himself in the sights

00:04:37.490 --> 00:04:42.490
of Javier Bardem's Anton Chigurh, a killer
sent to track down the missing money.

00:04:42.490 --> 00:04:47.560
Bardem is truly chilling as the ruthless psychopath
— just one of many standout performances

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:52.110
in a masterful film from cinematic visionaries
Joel and Ethan Coen.

00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:53.590
The Prestige

00:04:53.590 --> 00:04:58.169
Christopher Nolan took a short break from
Batman in 2006 and released a movie about

00:04:58.169 --> 00:05:02.300
a rival pair of stage magicians struggling
to stay relevant in the late 1800s.

00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:07.900
If that sounds like a total departure from
Nolan's usual work, rest assured — it isn't.

00:05:07.900 --> 00:05:13.229
The Prestige feels almost like a bridge connecting
Nolan's earlier, low-key films like Memento

00:05:13.229 --> 00:05:18.130
and Insomnia to his later, visually stunning
extravaganzas.

00:05:18.130 --> 00:05:22.550
Sometimes disturbing, always enthralling,
and woven throughout with threads of pure

00:05:22.550 --> 00:05:28.550
cinematic magic, The Prestige might be the
best movie Christopher Nolan's ever made — and

00:05:28.550 --> 00:05:30.610
that's saying a lot.

00:05:30.610 --> 00:05:31.610
Chicken Run

00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:35.129
Is it the most original animated animal adventure?

00:05:35.129 --> 00:05:36.440
Not by a long shot.

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:37.449
Is it the best?

00:05:37.449 --> 00:05:40.169
Well, that's that's definitely open to debate.

00:05:40.169 --> 00:05:44.650
Helmed by the team behind Wallace &amp; Gromit
and Shaun the Sheep, Chicken Run revels in

00:05:44.650 --> 00:05:49.439
the same style of goofy stop-motion animation
paired with witty writing that made those

00:05:49.439 --> 00:05:50.990
series such hits.

00:05:50.990 --> 00:05:55.879
Featuring a rare voice acting turn by Mel
Gibson, it's one of those rare non-Disney

00:05:55.879 --> 00:06:01.410
or Pixar animated films that stands up nearly
twenty years after its release as a family-friendly

00:06:01.410 --> 00:06:03.240
classic.

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:04.240
Boyhood

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:09.240
Filmed over the course of 12 years with the
same cast, Boyhood tracks a child's life as

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:12.490
he develops from a young boy to a college-bound
adult.

00:06:12.490 --> 00:06:18.270
The boy, Mason, is played by Ellar Coltrane,
who was seven when filming started and 19

00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:20.120
by the time of its release.

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:23.590
You literally watch him age onscreen as the
story progresses.

00:06:23.590 --> 00:06:28.900
Similarly, his parents, played by Ethan Hawke
and Patricia Arquette, age alongside him.

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:33.520
The runtime can be a little intimidating — it
clocks in just under three hours — but Richard

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:37.979
Linklater's groundbreaking and heartbreaking
Boyhood is well worth the investment.

00:06:37.980 --> 00:06:40.520
"I wish I could use the bumpers."

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:41.620
"Bumper are for kids, y'know?

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:43.240
What are you, two-years-old?

00:06:43.900 --> 00:06:45.319
You don't want the bumpers.

00:06:45.319 --> 00:06:47.540
Life doesn't give you bumpers."

00:06:47.540 --> 00:06:48.540
Thanks for watching!

00:06:48.540 --> 00:06:50.759
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:50.759 --> 00:06:52.970
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

